    "description": "Iran’s springs and waterfalls reveal connections between groundwater, mountain geology, and surface drainage. Zagros karst springs, the spring-fed cascades of Bisheh, and the mineral terraces of Badab-e Surt illustrate distinct ways that water shapes a landscape.",
    "tags": ["Springs", "Waterfalls", "Karst", "Groundwater", "Zagros", "Mineral terraces"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:16:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:16:48Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Springs & Waterfalls | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the groundwater and geology behind Zagros springs, Margoon and Bisheh waterfalls, and Badab-e Surt’s mineral terraces."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "underground-geography",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Where underground water becomes visible",
        "content": "A spring is a place where groundwater emerges at the surface; a waterfall is a drop in a flowing surface-water course. The two can occur together. In central and southern Iran, a field-based geological account describes large springs and waterfalls within karst terrain, where water circulates through soluble rock. The visible outlet is only one part of a system that also includes recharge areas, underground pathways, and geological barriers. [s1]\n\nKarst helps explain why a substantial spring need not have an obvious surface stream immediately above it. Limestone formations can carry groundwater, while less permeable layers influence where it moves and emerges. In the Zagros, folded rocks and faults complicate that underground geography. A spring’s contributing area cannot safely be inferred by drawing a small boundary around its visible pool. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "zagros-springs",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading a Zagros spring",
        "content": "Research on Sousan Spring in the central Zagros combined geological observations, water chemistry, stable isotopes, and a water balance to investigate its recharge area. The inspected abstract identifies limestone as the likely recharge formation and a major fault as an influence on the regional groundwater route. Stable isotopes are forms of chemical elements that can help researchers distinguish water sources and recharge conditions. [s2]\n\nThis example shows why discharge alone cannot explain a spring. Researchers also need to establish where its water originates and how rock structure redirects it. The findings describe Sousan’s investigated system; they should not be transferred unchanged to every spring in Iran. Different outlets can drain different formations even within the same mountain region. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "cascades-and-terraces",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Waterfalls and mineral terraces",
        "content": "Margoon, in southern Iran, is described in the geological field account as a waterfall produced by springs draining limestone. It makes the connection between groundwater and a dramatic surface feature unusually visible: water emerges along the rock face before descending as a cascade. [s1]\n\nBisheh in Lorestan offers another spring-fed example. Iran’s tourism portal describes springs above the waterfall and drainage onward to the Sezar River, a tributary of the Dez. The waterfall therefore belongs to a larger river network rather than forming an isolated scenic feature. Its setting also includes a village and the railway corridor through the Zagros. [s3]\n\nBadab-e Surt in Mazandaran presents a different landscape. Here mineral springs have created stepped pools and colored deposits down a slope. The tourism source attributes the reddish and orange coloration of one spring to iron oxide and describes contrasting water characteristics between the springs. These terraces are depositional features: the water helps build the visible landform. The source’s therapeutic claims are not used as medical evidence. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "what-the-view-cannot-show",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a photograph cannot establish",
        "content": "A photograph can show a flowing cascade, but it cannot establish annual discharge, drinking-water quality, or dependable flow in another season. Even the dimensions reported for famous waterfalls vary between publications. This account therefore emphasizes documented processes and drainage connections rather than rankings or precise heights. Understanding a spring or waterfall begins with its water source and geological setting; its appearance is the visible result of that larger system. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Popular Hydrogeology: Carbonate and Evaporitic Karst of Central and Southern Iran",
        "publisher": "Serbian Geological Society; hosted by the University of Belgrade Centre for Karst Hydrogeology",
        "url": "https://www.karst.edu.rs/documents/pdf/Karst%20of%20Southern%20Iran%20by%20Z%20Stevanovic%20-%20from%20Zapisnici%20SGD%20.pdf",
