    "level": 2,
    "parent_path": "/explore/geography/",
    "entity_type": "category",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s vegetation ranges from humid Caspian forests to dry mountain woodland, plateau shrublands, highland pastures, and tidal mangroves. Climate, elevation, soils, and human land use help explain both this diversity and the different conservation challenges facing each landscape.",
    "tags": ["Forests", "Vegetation", "Hyrcanian forests", "Zagros", "Rangelands", "Mangroves", "Plant diversity"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:16:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:16:48Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Forests & Vegetation | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Iran’s Caspian forests, Zagros woodland, Arasbaran landscapes, southern mangroves, rangelands, and native plant communities."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "vegetation-geography",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A landscape of contrasts",
        "content": "Iran’s vegetation is best understood as a mosaic shaped by water, altitude, and land use. Humid slopes facing the Caspian Sea support broadleaved forests, while much of the interior carries vegetation adapted to strong seasonal or persistent water deficits. Mountain relief changes rainfall and temperature over relatively short distances, creating different habitats between lowlands, slopes, and high summits. [s1]\n\nForest is only part of this story. Shrublands, steppe, alpine vegetation, and rangelands occupy important ecological and cultural positions. An open landscape is not automatically a destroyed forest: some plant communities naturally reflect aridity, salinity, or a short mountain growing season. Conversely, sparse cover cannot always be explained by climate alone, because grazing and cultivation have also altered vegetation. Interpretation requires attention to both environmental conditions and land-use history. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "hyrcanian",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Hyrcanian forests: the humid Caspian arc",
        "content": "The Hyrcanian forests form a broadleaved forest belt along the southern and southwestern Caspian region, extending into Azerbaijan as well as Iran. Their significance includes the survival of plant communities whose wider distributions contracted during past glacial periods. These forests acted as refuges from which vegetation could expand again when conditions became more favorable. The antiquity concerns the ecosystem’s evolutionary history, not the age of every tree standing today. [s2]\n\nUNESCO’s World Heritage property protects selected components of this larger forest region. Its inscription should therefore not be read as a designation covering every Hyrcanian woodland. UNESCO also identifies continuing pressures from seasonal grazing and wood collection, and emphasizes ecological connections between protected components. Formal protection and effective management are related but separate questions. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "zagros-and-arasbaran",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Zagros and Arasbaran: different mountain landscapes",
        "content": "The Zagros vegetation region belongs to a drier mountain setting than the humid Caspian belt. FAO’s country study treats it as a distinct forestry region rather than extending the Hyrcanian model across western Iran. Its account also connects mountain rangelands with seasonal livestock movements, showing why woodland conservation and pastoral livelihoods need to be considered together. [s3]\n\nArasbaran, in northwestern Iran, adds another combination of habitats. UNESCO’s biosphere description places it between the Caspian, Caucasian, and Mediterranean regions and describes forests alongside high alpine meadows, semi-arid steppe, rangelands, rivers, and springs. Its ecological interest lies partly in this variety rather than in an uninterrupted blanket of trees. [s4]\n\nThe same UNESCO account identifies agriculture, livestock husbandry, horticulture, beekeeping, handicrafts, and tourism among local activities. These are inhabited landscapes. Understanding conservation consequently requires asking how land uses fit together, which habitats they affect, and how local people participate, rather than assuming that a protected-area boundary removes livelihood pressures. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "southern-mangroves",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Hara: a forest shaped by tides",
        "content": "Southern Iran introduces a fundamentally different forest environment. At Hara, near Qeshm Island and the Strait of Khuran, UNESCO describes an Avicennia mangrove ecosystem associated with the Mehran River delta. Its setting includes tidal marshes, shallow coves, small islands, and marine habitat. [s5]\n\nMangroves belong within the vegetation story, but their environmental setting is coastal and tidal. Comparing them directly with mountain woodland solely by tree cover would conceal important differences. The surrounding wetland and shallow-water habitats are part of the ecological system, not simply empty space between forest patches. Hara therefore also connects this subject with Iran’s islands, coasts, and marine biodiversity. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "rangelands-and-native-plants",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Rangelands and the vegetation between forests",
        "content": "FAO’s historical country study distinguishes upland summer grazing areas from lower winter and autumn ranges. It also describes rangelands as natural grassland, shrubland, or combinations of both. The term therefore denotes more than a uniform grassy pasture. These landscapes support livelihoods while requiring management suited to their seasonal productivity. [s3]\n\nNative plant communities likewise differ across environments. Iran’s ecological literature distinguishes salt-tolerant vegetation in saline basins, steppe communities in dry country, and specialized high-mountain plants. A national list of native species is useful, but explaining a plant’s habitat and geographic distribution makes that list more meaningful. Species richness, vegetation cover, and habitat condition measure different things. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "conservation-and-measurement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Measure condition as well as area",
        "content": "Forest area alone cannot establish whether a landscape is ecologically secure. UNESCO’s Hyrcanian assessment highlights connectivity, grazing, wood collection, and enforcement alongside protected status. FAO’s older country study also warns of shortcomings in forest and rangeland information and distinguishes planted trees from natural vegetation. Its historical totals should not be presented as a current inventory. [s2][s3]\n\nThe useful conclusion is regional: humid forest, dry woodland, rangeland, and mangrove require different interpretations and management questions. The linked guides examine these environments separately while retaining the connections between vegetation, water, terrain, and livelihoods. [s1][s3][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s5"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Ecology",
        "publisher": "Encyclopaedia Iranica",
        "url": "https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/ecology/",
